{"author":{"id":"e0b5a45ee94df92761e1991b03f72f45cac91cde564e2faac1223bf52a3fe292"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788181228,"nonce":"99kyKotgZK8S4sE1rVa9s5qGJUg=","message":"HANDOVER — orchestrator session 6 (BORGES) ends at Evan's call, 2026-08-31\nmorning. **Work moves to dorian's Linux box.** Everything is pushed: main,\n`spec/3.4-pawn-orders`, `spec/1.8-game-clock-advance`, and all bug refs.\n\n**14 of 25 done** (two new issues filed this session, so the denominator moved).\n3.2 closed. RUNLOG's session-6 section has the full account.\n\nTWO OF EVAN'S CHALLENGES CHANGED THE BUILD, and both are now issues\n\n- **1.8 (b8785e8), state:next, p1** — `advance` should UNPAUSE the game and let\n  RimWorld tick, not pin `Paused` and pump `DoSingleTick` under a wall-clock\n  budget. Evan pushed on this and was right; my three defences (TimeSlower\n  immunity, thermal governor, exact tick counts) do not survive scrutiny and the\n  issue body records why, so nobody re-litigates it. The good argument is for\n  unpausing: `TickManager.Paused` includes `WindowStack.WindowsForcePause`, so\n  unpaused, VANILLA stops the clock itself when a modal goes up and\n  `LetterStack.OpenAutomaticLetters` stops being starved by construction — which\n  is the whole reason 1.7 needed a per-tick guard. One real commit is on the\n  branch (`fa11564`) and it is sound; the acceptance script beside it is\n  UNVERIFIED DRAFT, committed only so it was not lost.\n- **1.9 (acee526), state:next, p1, BLOCKS 3.3** — placement slides silently.\n  `ThingPlaceMode.Near` moves a blocked thing to a neighbouring cell and returns\n  `ok:true, placed:N` with no flag. Reproduced twice live. Outright FAILURE is\n  reported well already; this is only the quiet-success-elsewhere case. It blocks\n  3.3 because `place-layout` that nudges blueprints builds a house that is not the\n  one asked for, and the agent then plans against a floor plan that no longer\n  describes the world. 3.3 raised to p1 to sit beside it.\n\n3.2 (57ab92a) — CLOSED, merged `ccb8d67`, DLL `2558b08`\n\nSix verbs, all three bullets driven live. Highlights: 25 trees from one envelope\nover 520 cells with wood and designation counts both confirming pawns took the\njobs; the stockpile proven with VANILLA HAULING as the independent actor after I\nthrew away a first attempt that had contaminated its own evidence; and the flick\ntrap, where `Knowledge.xml` reads `SwitchFlickingDesignation` = 0 so vanilla\nWOULD have wedged the run on the colony's first power switch — a measured\ncounterfactual rather than an argument. One orchestrator fix during acceptance\n(`037c5df`): the spec's own bullet-2 call was impossible because the shared\nresolver ate `filter` as a target selector. Full evidence and an explicit\nnot-demonstrated list are on the issue.\n\n3.4 (39c9db7) — BUILT AND PUSHED, **NOT VERIFIED**, state:next\n\n35 verbs, 8 new files, clean build, 78-envelope acceptance script committed —\n**none of it has run in the game.** Take this first on the box with a bench. Two\nthings the worker found that the next orchestrator needs:\n\n- It fixed a real bug on self-review: `int.MaxValue * 2` wrapped negative and\n  aborted the work-giver scan before its first giver, so `prioritize` would have\n  failed EVERY call and bullet 2 outright.\n- **Acceptance bullet 3's stated mechanism does not exist.** `dev:weather` cannot\n  drive a re-dress: `neededWarmth` comes from the tile's SEASONAL average\n  (`JobGiver_OptimizeApparel` -\u003e `CalculateNeededWarmth(pawn, tile,\n  GenLocalDate.Twelfth)`), verified. The clothes loop is filter-driven and\n  season-independent — the bullet's intent is testable, its method is stale.\n\nOrchestrator debt at 3.4's merge: fold its `Pawn_ReadingTracker` guarded route\ninto `PawnSafe.Policies` (reading policies postdate 2.2, field `reading`), and\ndedupe its private `action` emitter against 3.2's.\n\n3.5 AND 3.6 ARE PRE-ARMED — read the recon comments before dispatching\n\nBoth carry verified decompiled-source recon, re-checked by me at file+line:\n\n- **3.5 (20e5cda), state:next, p1, M1-CRITICAL.** The load-bearing answer: a\n  trade CAN be set up and executed with NO window on the stack, so the\n  transact-the-model invariant survives. **But `TradeDeal.TryExecute`'s\n  cannot-afford branch calls `Find.WindowStack.WindowOfType\u003cDialog_Trade\u003e()\n  .FlashSilver()` unguarded — an NRE on exactly the headless path.** Pre-validate\n  so the branch is unreachable; do not try/catch it. `TradeSession.Close()` has\n  zero vanilla callers. `DiaOption.Activate()` does NOT check `disabled` (the UI\n  checks it one line earlier, as an argument to `Widgets.ButtonText`), so a\n  faithful replay would press greyed-out buttons. Exactly three letter classes\n  break the options contract.\n- **3.6 (48f666c).** `BillStack.AddBill` really is four lines and validates\n  NOTHING; the gate to reproduce is recipe-in-AllRecipes + `AvailableNow` +\n  `AvailableOnNow(table)` + `Count \u003c 15`. **`AvailableNow` is itself a\n  write-on-read** and `WorldSafe` has no recipe-level route yet — this spec owes\n  `WorldSafe.RecipeAvailableNow`. The storage-group shadowing trap is real.\n\nTHREE HAZARD CLASSES ADDED TO DESIGN THIS SESSION\n\n1. A vanilla helper can end in a tutorial modal, and a modal is a force-pause.\n2. **Correction to my own entry, found by 3.4's worker:** I grepped for\n   `DoModalDialogIfNotKnown`; the real query is `new Dialog_MessageBox`. Sixth\n   site is `HealthCardUtility.CreateSurgeryBill(sendMessages: true)` — default\n   TRUE — from the ordinary bill path, and `ITab_Bills` reaches the same helper,\n   so **3.6 inherits it.** The taxonomy that matters: behind a FloatMenuOption's\n   action closure is SAFE while we never invoke delegates; on a utility's own\n   execution path is DANGEROUS.\n3. **Write-on-SAVE.** `Bill.ExposeData` narrows a bill's live `ingredientFilter`\n   during the SAVING pass. In DESIGN rather than a code comment because it\n   invalidates a METHOD: save-and-read-the-Scribe-XML was 2.2's and 2.4's\n   independent second reader, and for this surface it perturbs what it measures.\n\nBENCH STATE ON BORGES\n\nHealthy, 3 boots, 0 red errors in every session. **`autostart.rws` is still\narmed** from kit-accept.rws — delete it or the next launch boots into a fixture.\nScratch state left on that save: a claimed bed, a standing lamp, two stockpiles,\na sown rice zone. Both worker worktrees still exist on this box; their branches\nare on GitHub, so nothing is lost if the box is wiped.\n\nRECOMMENDED ORDER ON THE LINUX BOX: 3.4 verification (needs only a bench), then\n1.9, then 1.8 resumed from `fa11564`, then 3.5 and 3.6.","files":null}]}